<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Walking in the Afternoon.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about people or things using simple language. Interactive: Discuss homework assignments with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about people, using simple descriptions (e.g., "My friend can swim."). Can discuss homework assignments with peers by offering or requesting help and following up with clarifying questions (e.g., "I need help with my physical education homework." "I can help you with the instructions." "What part do you need help with?"). Can ask classmates about their homework (e.g., "What is the homework for our physical education class?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "What Can You Do?" activity. The teacher shows flashcards of different exercises/sports (e.g., swimming, running, dancing, cycling, playing football, hiking, walking). Students respond "I can [activity]" or "I cannot [activity]". For example, teacher shows "swimming" card, students say "I can swim!" or "I cannot swim." Teacher encourages students to use target vocabulary such as 'swim', 'run', 'dance', 'play', 'hike', 'walk'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences: "I like walking in the afternoon." "My friend can swim." "Do you like cycling?" "Yes, I do. I ride my bike every day." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim," exaggerating the sounds. The teacher also models segmenting 'exciting' (ex-cit-ing) and 'wellness' (well-ness).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of preferences and abilities.
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I can swim,' does it mean I go to the gym or that I know how to swim?" (It means you know how to swim.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I ask 'Do you like running?', am I asking about food or an activity?" (An activity.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short dialogue between two friends, Leo and Maria, planning an after-school exercise activity and briefly discussing their PE homework. The dialogue is written on the board or projected, and an audio recording can be played.
                    <br><em>Leo: "Hi Maria! Do you like sports?"</em>
                    <br><em>Maria: "Yes, I do! I like cycling every day. What about you?"</em>
                    <br><em>Leo: "I like running. I run sometimes in the afternoon. Do you want to go to the park this afternoon? We can run together!"</em>
                    <br><em>Maria: "That sounds exciting! I can run with you. Oh, wait! What is the homework for our physical education class? I need help with the instructions."</em>
                    <br><em>Leo: "It's about healthy activities. I can help you with the instructions. What part do you need help with?"</em>
                    <br><em>Maria: "Thanks, friend! Let's talk about it at the park!"</em>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking about preferences: "Do you like to swim?" Expressing preferences: "I like swimming." Encouraging others: Suggesting group activities to encourage and support peers (e.g., "Let's run together!" "You can do it!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue and answer orally:
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of exercise does Maria like?" (She likes cycling.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where do Leo and Maria plan to go?" (To the park.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Maria need help with her homework?" (Yes, she does.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Modal verbs (can, cannot), Present simple questions and affirmative sentences (Do you like sports? Yes, I do.), WH-question + answer + subject + verb + ing (What type of exercise do you like? I like swimming.).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: walk, exercise, afternoon, sport, activity, friend, football, swimming, running, hiking, dancing, cycling, play, run, jump, dance, healthy, active, every day, sometimes.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim", segmenting "exciting".</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> Students work individually to complete sentences orally or write them in their notebooks using "can/cannot" or "like/likes" and target verbs/nouns. Teacher provides sentence starters on the board:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I ___ (swim / cannot swim) in the ocean.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My friend ___ (like / likes) to play football.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Do you ___ (like / likes) dancing? Yes, I ___.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What type of activity do you ___ (like / likes)? I ___ (like / likes) hiking.</li>
                            </ul>
                            Teacher checks answers orally, focusing on correct grammar and pronunciation.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice – Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a model sentence, e.g., "I like running every day." Students then substitute parts of the sentence using flashcards or a list of vocabulary words (e.g., swimming, cycling, dancing, football, walking, afternoon, sometimes, always). Partner A says a sentence, Partner B repeats and changes one word. Example:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "I like running every day."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "I like <u>swimming</u> every day."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "I like swimming <u>sometimes</u>."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "I like <u>playing football</u> sometimes."</li>
                            </ul>
                            Teacher monitors and provides feedback on pronunciation, especially /k/ and /s/.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction – Q&A Chain:</b> Students stand in a circle. The teacher starts by asking a student, "Do you like [activity]?" The student answers and then asks the next student, "What type of exercise do you like?" That student answers and asks another, creating a chain. They use words like 'friend', 'sport', 'activity'. Teacher encourages using "can/cannot" in their answers.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs or small groups (3 students). Students will act out a conversation planning an after-school exercise activity and discussing their physical education homework. This connects to the theme "I Like Walking in the Afternoon." and the scenario "Time for Exercise."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario Introduction:</b> The teacher sets the scene: "Imagine you meet your friend after school. You want to plan an exercise activity for the afternoon. Also, one of you has a question about the physical education homework and needs help, or wants to offer help."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Planning Phase (5 minutes):</b> In pairs/groups, students decide:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What activity will they do? (e.g., walking, running, cycling, playing football)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who needs help with homework, or who will offer help?</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should aim to use phrases like: "Do you like...?", "I like...", "What type of exercise do you like?", "I can...", "Let's [activity] together!", "You can do it!", "What is the homework?", "I need help with...", "I can help you with...", "What part do you need help with?"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role-play (5-7 minutes per pair/group):</b> Students perform their role-play for another pair or for the class. They should focus on fluency and communicating their ideas effectively, using the target language. The teacher observes, taking notes for feedback, but intervenes only if communication completely breaks down.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Encouragement:</b> The teacher reminds students to encourage their friends during the activity planning and when discussing homework.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the role-play scenario created in Stage 4, demonstrating their ability to talk about exercise preferences, plan an activity, and discuss homework with peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key words (e.g., /k/ in "can", /s/ in "swim") and segmenting multisyllabic words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal verbs "can/cannot" correctly when talking about abilities.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks and answers "Do you like...?" questions about sports/activities.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses WH-questions to ask about types of exercise or homework ("What type of exercise...?", "What is the homework?", "What part...?")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Offers or requests help for homework ("I need help...", "I can help you...").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses encouraging phrases ("Let's run together!", "You can do it!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Actively listens and responds to partner.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I can use to talk about exercise is: _______."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can (circle one: easily / with some help / with a lot of help) ask my friend about their homework."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about exercise or homework was easy for me today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I want to practice more to speak better?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific speaking activities worked best to encourage active participation and interaction?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to successfully use modal verbs ("can/cannot") and present simple questions/answers in natural conversation?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they integrate the homework discussion smoothly into the role-play, and offer/request help? What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students struggling with fluency or specific grammar points?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I ensure more consistent use of the pronunciation focus (/k/, /s/, segmenting) in future speaking tasks?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4 – Writing Composition):</b> For the next lesson, students will write short sentences or paragraphs describing their favorite activities and their friends' abilities, building on the speaking practice from today. They will also write short notes offering or requesting help with homework.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>